Subject: ios: a master gain on the frame's audio buffer, and a queue that never starves

Three things the iOS shell needs from port/src/audio.c, all PLATFORM_IOS-guarded
so every other platform is byte for byte upstream's.

1. `pdPlatformAudioGain()` - a weak hook multiplied into the s16 buffer just
   before SDL_QueueAudio. It carries the settings page's Volume and Mute rows
   and the "Other App Audio" duck (app/ios/PDAudio.m, D-033). The game's own
   Sound/Music/Sound Mode stay exactly where they are: in the eeprom, edited in
   the game's Audio Options page. A gain of 1.0 costs one float compare.

2. A queue instrument. audioEndFrame() queues ONE rendered frame's samples per
   frame while the device drains on the clock, so a load hitch is a production
   gap with nothing banked behind it. That is the user's intro stutter, and no
   counter this port had could see it. g_PdAudio* are read over the bridge,
   including what SDL actually opened and the samples per push.

3. The iOS buffer floors, a cushion queued ONCE, and a rate matcher
   (M-029/M-030/M-035/M-036, D-039). A
   512-sample device buffer is 23 ms, less than one 60 Hz frame of slack, and
   the queue starts empty. 1024 samples of device buffer, a 16384-sample queue
   limit, and 1408 samples of silence (one device buffer plus one frame of
   production) laid down at the first push.

   Round B had that cushion self-healing - re-queued whenever the queue dipped
   below one device buffer - and that was wrong in a way the simulator could not
   show: the sawtooth's floor is below a device buffer by construction, so the
   top-up fired on every pull and inserted 56 ms of silence three times a second
   for the whole session (1340 refills, 1.66 M samples of silence in five
   minutes on the user's Air). It IS the stutter he reported. The cushion is laid
   once now, and re-laid only after the queue has actually reached zero (D-035).

4. A hitch handled as a discontinuity (D-078). The engine renders a whole
   hitch's audio in one frame when it catches up (diffframe60 ticks at once),
   and the old +/-10 % loop chased that burst for 10-15 s with its integrator
   wound to the clamp: the user heard every stutter as seconds of high, fast
   sound. Now the loop's authority is 1 % (a stored 10 is clamped), it works on
   a filtered level with its integrator frozen outside the working band, an
   underrun re-primes once with a fade-in, and a burst is faded out and dropped
   back to the setpoint in one step. A per-push trace ring (`audio trace` on the
   bridge) records queue, ratio, integrator and every resync.

diff --git a/port/src/audio.c b/port/src/audio.c
index 791b5f8ab..63ed859b0 100644
--- a/port/src/audio.c
+++ b/port/src/audio.c
@@ -15,6 +15,408 @@ static s32 bufferSize = 512;
 static s32 queueLimit = 8192;
